From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] local: arm64: dts: qcom: hamoa: drop the TCSR download-mode cookie so the XO can be released

The scm node's qcom,dload-mode = <&tcsr 0x19000> makes qcom_scm look the TCSR
up as a syscon. of_syscon_register() takes the node's first clock -- for the
TCSR that is RPMH_CXO_CLK, the XO reference the clkref gates are parented to --
and regmap_mmio_attach_clk() holds a clk_prepare() on it for the life of the
regmap. clk-rpmh casts its vote on prepare, not enable, and for the non-AO
bi_tcxo that vote goes into the active, wake and sleep sets alike. The APSS
therefore tells AOP that the crystal must stay on while it sleeps, forever, and
AOSS deep sleep (XO shutdown) is off the table before anything else is looked at.

Measured on the Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x (X1E-80-100) with every other XO consumer
traced across an s2idle: bi_tcxo awake reads enable 8, prepare 9, and its named
handles are the two attach-only remoteprocs (never prepared), the USB combo PHY
(released in suspend) and "clock-controller@1fc0000, no connection id" -- the
syscon's of_clk_get(np, 0). Inside the suspend every child of bi_tcxo was
unprepared and bi_tcxo itself was disabled, but never unprepared, and xo.lvl
was never written.

The cookie exists to request a crash dump in download mode. download_mode
defaults to off, in which case qcom_scm_set_download_mode() does nothing
without an address, and nothing else on this machine references the TCSR as a
syscon. Drop it. The clkref gates keep their parent through the clock
provider's own regmap, which attaches no clock.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
@@ -330,7 +330,6 @@
 			compatible = "qcom,scm-x1e80100", "qcom,scm";
 			interconnects = <&aggre2_noc MASTER_CRYPTO QCOM_ICC_TAG_ALWAYS
 					 &mc_virt SLAVE_EBI1 QCOM_ICC_TAG_ALWAYS>;
-			qcom,dload-mode = <&tcsr 0x19000>;
 		};
 
 		scmi {
